# Port scope and evidence

The exact package and engine source commits are sourceCommit and engineCommit
in HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json, which is generated at export. This document states
what the port implements, what has been compared and what is untested; it is
not a build or compatibility result for the exported package, which needs its
own checks. The reference runtime is Decko 0.20.0 / card 1.110.0 on Ruby
3.2.3 on the project's Linux VM, where the synthetic-v1 recorded Ruby outputs
were captured on 2026-11-03; EVIDENCE.md states the reference each quoted
result was measured against. Another version or deployment needs
its own comparison. This is an independent TypeScript port of Decko, offered
under GPL-3.0-or-later; see NOTICE.

The implementation includes card names and inflections, row fetching, rule and
permission lookup, CQL parsing and SQL generation, views and nesting, history,
card saves and events, account/session handling, attachments, assets and
hand-ported mod registrations. RUBY-MAP.md, generated at export, lists each
TypeScript module and its Ruby citations, including uncited modules rather
than hiding them.

The compatibility contract has four independent categories: persistence,
semantics, rendering and side effects. Passing a render comparison says nothing
about an unexecuted save or notification. The packaged reference harness can
measure these categories on the included synthetic deck. No current aggregate
compatibility percentage is claimed by this package.

The recorded Node/SQLite synthetic-v1 grades pass rendering 1754/1754,
semantics 2389/2389, persistence 16/16 and side effects 19/19 against the
Ruby 3.2.3 reference. A native MySQL 8.4.11 full grade also passes all four
categories against a fresh stock Ruby 3.2.3 MySQL capture. The complete renamed
synthetic-v2 comparison passes with 2557 matching render results and 24
reproduced Ruby errors, not 24 mismatches. These measurements cover the exact
development snapshots named in [EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md); source changes since
the older historical measurements include behavior fixes, not just comments.
Combining passing branches or exporting their source does not by itself grade
the resulting package. Historical browser and database checks remain recorded
separately in that evidence document.

The complete mod-write probe remains a failing check. Its latest checkpoint
still has persistence, side-effect and other-table mismatches, even though its
rendering comparisons match. The remaining write defects must be repaired;
neither source presence nor a limited correction establishes full mod-write
compatibility or release readiness.

The HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json generated at export pins the engine and packaging
source actually included. A result for this source needs its own comparison
with a stated checker, source, fixture and settings; a local build or a
passing subset is not a substitute.

Source present is not supported deployment evidence. SQL adapter source includes
SQLite, libSQL HTTP, D1, MySQL and PostgreSQL; runtime-specific behavior must be
verified independently on each selected adapter. On 2026-11-02 the runtime
database matrix's five assertions (saves, rollback, concurrent writes,
isolated concurrent reads and permissions) passed 5 of 5 against a
PostgreSQL 16.15 server from the `postgres:16-alpine` container image on a
Linux machine, with the original assertions run under `node:test` rather than
Vitest; and 5 of 5 under Vitest against MySQL 8.4.11 on a Windows x64
workstation; on the same workstation PGlite passed 3, with the 2
concurrency assertions skipped because the embedded database offers only one
session, a limit of PGlite rather than a port defect. The source tree,
machine and test output for each are in [EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md). This
package's local commands use Node and SQLite only. The export's file
selection is designed to exclude hosted-provider credentials, deployment
resource configuration and production data. Native Bun and hosted-provider
acceptance are outside the package smoke check.

The package contains TypeScript implementations and registrations for the 35
Ruby mod gems, the card core and the TypeScript-only marketplace. The registry
records their presence; it is not proof that every write or side effect matches
Ruby. A third-party mod written in Ruby for upstream Decko must be ported
to TypeScript before use, because no host this package deploys to runs Ruby.
The source also holds an experimental loader that evaluates a mod's Ruby set
files under Ruby compiled to WebAssembly (optional dependencies, used by no
host); its presence does not establish arbitrary Ruby gem, native extension,
Rails or HAML compatibility. Inspect the loader interfaces and test the
specific mod.
The TypeScript mod DSL supports declared synchronous format helpers, view
permission predicates and set-scoped registration. It does not synthesize
every Ruby trait accessor or make arbitrary Ruby method names override
renderer internals. Each engine owns its compiled fallback view table;
registration in another loader is not shared runtime state for that engine.
The redistributable development suite and reference inputs are included.
Private migration corpora and development-only tooling tests are excluded;
four captures with unproven input origins and their three runtime readers are
also excluded, while 29 public-source tests remain included. Unknown origin
does not establish private origin. A missing test or fixture is not a passing test. The tests
that need private or absent inputs, the two untested trial-database checks and
the hosted comparison that real-data behaviour rests on (a Ruby Decko site
used as the comparison reference, against a hosted deployment of the port on
the Bunny (bunny.net) edge platform) are listed in
[UNTESTED-INPUTS.md](UNTESTED-INPUTS.md).

This package is not a claim that all engine work has closed. Later development
can change the engine; the manifest pins the code actually included in this
package.
